2 CONT
2 CONT * https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ливен,_Анатолий_Павлович
2 CONT
2 CONT His Serene Highness Prince Anatoly Pavlovich Lieven (November 16 (28), 1872[1], St. Petersburg - April 3, 1937, Kemeri) was a Russian officer, a member of the White movement. Colonel (1919)[1]. One of the leaders of the Brotherhoo
2 CONC d of Russian Truth. He participated in the Struggle for the Independence of Latvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cavalry guard A. P. Lieven in 1898
2 CONT of the Lutheran faith. He came from the nobility of the St. Petersburg province from a family of Baltic Germans of the Lieven family. He was the son of the chief master of ceremonies Prince Pavel Ivanovich Lieven (1821-1881) and h
2 CONC is wife Natalia Feodorovna von der Pahlen (1842-1920).
2 CONT
2 CONT He graduated from the St. Petersburg 3rd Gymnasium (1891) and the Faculty of Law of St. Petersburg University (1895). After graduating from the university, on September 4, 1895, he entered the Cavalry Guards Regiment as a cadet. H
2 CONC aving passed the officer exam at the Nikolaev Cavalry School, on September 7, 1896, he was promoted to cornet.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1898, he entered the reserve of the Guards Cavalry and settled in his estate of Mezoten. In 1900-1901 he was an indispensable member of the Courland provincial office for peasant affairs. In 1901, he was granted the rank of Cha
2 CONC mber Junker.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1909, he was elected chairman of the Committee of the Council of the Russian Evangelical Union[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1912-1914 he was the Bauska district marshal of the nobility[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT With the outbreak of the First World War, he returned to the Cavalry Guards Regiment. He was awarded the Order of St. George of the 4th degree
2 CONT
2 CONT For the fact that in the battle of August 31, 1915, being sent with his platoon to assist the squadron in the attack on the village of Yakyani from the south, he secretly deployed his platoon, dashingly and unexpectedly attacked t
2 CONC he enemy, superior in strength at least half a squadron, knocked him out of the village of Yakyany, and himself hacked to death an officer and several lower ranks; Most of the enemy was cut down here, the Germans rushed to flee. C
2 CONC arrying away the lower ranks of the platoon with his bravery, he broke into the village of Abkharty on the shoulders of the retreating enemy, where he cut down the baggage train, captured the field kitchen and packs with documents
2 CONC  and maps. With his dashing attack, cornet Prince Lieven contributed to the success of the general offensive.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 19, 1915 he was promoted to lieutenant. On September 19, 1916, he was promoted to staff captain. By 1917 he was a captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 18, 1918, he was arrested with his family. A. P. Lieven wrote in his memoirs: "... the day before the start of the German offensive on Pskov and Narva... the Bolsheviks arrested me with my wife and underage daughter."[
2 CONC 3] Among the 161 hostages, he was sent to Yekaterinburg and imprisoned. In March 1918, according to the Brest-Litovsk Peace Treaty, hostages from the Baltic States were handed over to the Germans in Orsha[3]. After his release fro
2 CONC m captivity, he returned to the Baltic States and took part in the Latvian War of Independence as part of the Baltic Landeswehr.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1919 he formed and headed the Libava Volunteer Rifle Detachment in Libau, which, together with units of the Baltic Landeswehr of von der Goltz, at the end of May 1919 drove the Bolsheviks out of Riga, which they had pre
2 CONC viously occupied. After the occupation of Riga, on May 24, 1919, Colonel Lieven's detachment, pursuing the retreating Bolsheviks, was ambushed, in which Lieven was seriously wounded in the thigh and abdomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although most of the Landeswehr fought against the independent Republic of Latvia after the refusal of the Latvians to recognize the pro-German regime of A. Niedra, in May 1919 the units of the Landeswehr subordinate to A. Lieven 
2 CONC recognized the independence of Latvia and went over to the side of the Latvian armed forces. Lieven considered it principled, for the sake of the common struggle against the Bolsheviks, to support the independence of the Baltic st
2 CONC ates and to abandon the principle of "one indivisible Russia"[5]. The same position was supported by Captain Dydorov, who replaced Lieven during his treatment for his wounds. Later, Lieven's supporters enjoyed respect in independe
2 CONC nt Latvia and Estonia, where their association existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT After treatment, Lieven arrived to join the North-Western Army. From the summer to December 1919 he was commander of the 5th Infantry Division, into which Lieven's detachment was reorganized.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the end of the war, he lived in the Republic of Latvia[K 1], became a brick manufacturer. In 1921, he participated in the Reichenhall Monarchist Congress, headed the Latvian branch of the Brotherhood of Russian Truth. In 193
2 CONC 1 he headed the Society for Mutual Assistance of Military Personnel in Latvia. In 1934, he took the post of Brother No 1 at the Brotherhood of Russian Truth. The organization split, and part of it recognized Lieven as their leader
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in the publication of the collections "White Deed: Chronicle of the White Struggle", published by Gen. A. A. von Lampe (in 1926-1933 7 books were published). He also published a collection of memoirs "Memo of a Liv
2 CONC en, 1919 - 1929", dedicated to the history of his detachment. He published the journal "Communication Service of the Livny [and North-Westers]" (No 1 (November 1929) - No 8 (July 1936)).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in the church cemetery in Mežotnia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT He was married twice.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife (from June 28, 1897) was Her Serene Highness Princess Serafima Nikolaevna Saltykova (26.02.1875[7]—10.05.1898[8]), daughter of Chief Master of Ceremonies N. I. Saltykov; was born in St. Petersburg, baptized on April
2 CONC  6, 1875 in the Church of St. Great Martyr Panteleimon at the reception of Prince V. V. Golitsyn and grandmother Princess M. A. Dolgorukova; maid of honor of the court (1894), died of puerperal fever, buried in the estate of Mežot
2 CONC ne, Courland province. Their daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Seraphima (21.04.1898—22.12.1967)
2 CONT His second wife (from 3 September 1902) was Baroness Elisabeth-Jeannette Maria von Firks (17.02.1873-04.03.1941). Their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dina Antoinette (1903–1982), married in 1923 to Baron Kirill Pavlovich Gan (1897–1967)[9];
2 CONT Pavel German (1905-1965);
2 CONT Karl Johann (1911-1996).
2 CONT Go to the template "Wikidata/Ancestors" 
2 CONT Anatoly Pavlovich Lieven — ancestors
2 CONT Awards
2 CONT Order of St. Stanislaus, 3rd class with swords and bow;
2 CONT Order of St. Anna of the 4th degree with the inscription "For bravery" (VP 4.07.1916);
2 CONT Order of St. George, 4th class (VP 25.03.1916)
2 CONT Military Cross (Great Britain) (22.11.1915)
2 CONT Comments
2 CONT according to other sources, since 1920 he had been in exile in France, but since 1921 he lived in his estate of Mesotain. In 1924-1930 he was a plenipotentiary representative of the Brotherhood of Russian Truth in the Baltic State
2 CONC s. In 1927-1929 he was a personal resident of the head of the Russian All-Military Union [1].
