1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Children of Wiley and Diannah are Rebecca married Noah Hale Margaret married Granville Combs move to Taney Co, Mo Jane married John S. Amburgey Elizabeth married Alfred Amburgey Mary Polly married Elijah Sparkman A
2 CONC mbrose married Hannan Martin Mullins Susan married Silas Francis buried Taney Co, Mo John Jesse married Lucy Jane Hagins Robert H. married Lucinda Adams William Riley married Harriet Whitaker Diannah died and Wiley married Surilda
2 CONC  Madden abt 1884 and had 9 more children
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=102433669 Find A Grave Memorial] via brother [http://www.geni.com/profile-34665880917 Thomas Francis Amburgey] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18
2 CONC 783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 27 2014, 17:02:42 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT 111233 Children 19  Perry County KY in 1827  Littcarr KY, Letcher County in 1842, Hindman in 1884 Wiley Amburgey was brought into Kentucky in 1827, at the age of 4, when his father, Ambrose, moved here from North Carolina.  The fa
2 CONC mily settled on Little Carr Creek, in Knott County, and experienced the hardships and excitements of pioneer life.  Wiley died in 1916 at the age of 93.  
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the high spots in Wiley's life was his escape from a revenue officer.  Nearly everyone made whisky at that time and it was generally understood that no man would be molested for making it unless he was a personal enemy of t
2 CONC he marshal.  It was aalso understood that when a raid was made it was not for an arrest but to make a killing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiley must have incurred the ill will of the officers, for one day they appeared at his home and there was no chance for his escape.  A chest in which bed covers were usually kept was standing in one room.  It was a big, strong bo
2 CONC x of water birch, sawed by hand with a rip saw.  Quikcly the hunted man dropped into the chest and the lide was closed.  The marshal entered the house and , tiring of his vain search, sat down on the old chest to rest.  No sign wa
2 CONC s given by the family during that period of terrible suspense to betray the fugitive in his hiding place.  Wiley lay in galvanized immobility ready to leap to mortal combat at the lifting of the lid.  But the lid was not lifted.  
2 CONC The marshal rested for a few minutes over the human volcano and then went away.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The old chest is still a treasured possession of the Amburgey family.  (Reprinted in The Mountain Magazine from the Courier-Journal, April 11, 1948.)
